le Press, 1979), p. 62.\n\nMarcia Kay Stein, \u201cOrph\u00e9e, Eurydice et la vie contemporaine,\u201d in Chiara Stefani, Vincent Pomar\u00e8de, and G\u00e9rard de Wallens, Corot, un artiste et son temps (Paris: Klincksieck and Mus\u00e9e du Louvre and Rome: Acad\u00e9mie de France \u00e0 Rome, Villa M\u00e9dicis, 1998), pp. 287\u201388, 295 no. 20, p. 302, fig. 7. \n\nPeter L. Schmunk, \u201cMusic and the Art of Corot,\u201d Southeastern College Art Conference Review 13, 4 (1999), p. 361, n. 32. \n\nM. Therese Southgate, \u201cAbout the Cover: Wounded Eurydice,\u201d Journal of the American Medical Association 283, 22 (2000), p. 2899, cover ill. \n\nJanis P. Stout, \u201c\u2018The Nude Had Descended the Staircase\u2019: Katherine Anne Porter Looks at Willa Cather Looking at Modern Art,\u201d in Sarah Cheney Watson and Ann Moseley, eds., Willa Cather and Aestheticism: From Romanticism to Modernism (Madison: Fairleigh Dickinson University Press and Lanham, Maryland: Rowman & Littlefield, 2012), p. 160.","exhibition_history":"Paris, Durand-Ruel, R\u00e9trospective de tableaux et dessins des ma\u00eetres modernes, 1878, no. 59.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, A Century of Progress, June 1\u2013November 1, 1933, no. 235.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, A Century of Progress, June 1\u2013November 1, 1934, no. 172.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Corot, 1796\u20131875: An Exhibition of His Paintings and Graphic Works, October 6\u2013November 13, 1960, no. 117.\n\nTokyo, Seibu Museum of Art, Shikago Bijutsukan Insho-ha ten (The Impressionist Tradition: Masterpieces from the Art Institute of Chicago), October 18\u2013December 17, 1985, no. 8; Fukuoka Art Museum, January 5\u2013February 2, 1986; Kyoto Municipal Museum of Art, March 4\u2013April 13, 1986.","provenance_text":"Probably Alfred Sensier (died 1877), Paris [according to Robaut 1905]. Charles (?) Edwards, Paris, by 1878 [lent to Paris 1878]; sold H\u00f4tel Drouot, Paris, February 24, 1881, lot 8, as Eurydice for 6,510 francs to Durand-Ruel, possibly on behalf of Tavernier [price and buyer according to an annotated copy of the sale catalogue in the Rijksbureau voor Kunsthistorische Documentatie, The Hague]; Tavernier Collection, Paris [according to Robaut 1905, who states that it was acquired at the Edwards sale]. Henry Field (died 1890), Chicago; his widow Mrs. Florence Lathrop Field; given to the Art Institute, 1894.","is_public_domain":true,"artwork_type_title":"Painting","artist_title":"Jean Baptiste Camille Corot","style_title":"19th century","image_id":"f024968c-3cb1-f1cc-b1af-2a1d40b7e193","description":null},{"_score":null,"id":879,"title":"Landscape (The Lock)","date_start":1815,"date_end":1830,"date_display":"c. 1820\u201325","place_of_origin":"England","medium_display":"Oil on canvas","publication_history":"\u201cDrift of the Dog Days,\u201d The Collector 4, 16 (1893), p. 247.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Catalogue of Paintings, Sculpture, and Other Objects in the Museum (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1894), p. 55, no. 11. \n\n\u201cThe Henry Field Memorial Collection,\u201d Art Amateur 31, 6 (1894), p. 115. \n\n\u201cArt Institute of Chicago: II \u2013 Modern Paintings: The Henry Field Collection,\u201d Art Amateur 32, 1 (1894), p. 19. \n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Annual Report of the Trustees for the Year Ending June 4, 1895 (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1895), p. 37, no. 11. \n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Catalogue of the Objects in the Museum (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1895), p. 65, no. 11.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Catalogue of Objects in the Museum (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1896), p. 71, no. 11.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Catalogue of Objects in the Museum (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1898), p. 91, no. 11.\n\n\u201cChicago, ILL.: The Art Institute of Chicago,\u201d in American Art Annual 1898 (London: Macmillan & Co., 1899), p. 140, no. 11.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, General Catalogue of Objects in the Museum (Chicago: Art I