 136.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Handbook of Sculpture, Architecture and Paintings (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1922), p. 25, no. 136.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Handbook of Sculpture, Architecture, and Paintings (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1923), p. 25, no. 136.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, A Guide to the Paintings in the Permanent Collection (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1925), pp. 49 (ill.), 155, no. 136.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, A Guide to the Paintings in the Permanent Collection (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1932), pp. 45 (ill.), 174. \n\nFriedrich von Boetticher, Malerwerke des Neunzehnten Jahrhunderts, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Leipzig: Schmidt & G\u00fcnther, 1944), p. 901, no. 19.\n\nArt Institute of Chicago, Paintings in the Art Institute of Chicago: A Catalogue of the Picture Collection (Chicago: Art Institute of Chicago, 1961), p. 455.\n\nValentina B. Berezina, The Hermitage Catalogue of Western European Painting: French Painting: Early and Mid-Nineteenth Century (New York: Johnson Reprint\/Harcourt Brace Jovanovich, 1983), p. 451, under no. 419. \n\nEric Zafran, Santa Barbara Collects: Impressions of France, exh. cat. (Santa Barbara: Santa Barbara Museum of Art, 1998), p. 122, under no. 65.","exhibition_history":"Chicago, World\u2019s Columbian Exposition, Fine Arts: Painting, Sculpture, Architecture, Decoration, 1893, cat. 2983.","provenance_text":"E. Gaillard, Paris, by 1867; his sale for 7,000 francs [sale according to Narischkine sale catalogue, cited below; price according to Souill\u00e9 1900]. B. Narischkine, Paris, by 1883; his sale, Galerie George Petit, April 5, 1883, lot 83 as La Route du march\u00e9 for 42,500 francs to Arnold & Tripp [price and buyer according to an annotated copy of the sale catalogue in the Rijksbureau voor Kunsthistorische Documentatie, The Hague]; Arnold & Tripp, Paris. Henry Field (died 1890), Chicago; his widow Mrs. Florence Lathrop Field; given to the Art Institute, 1894.","is_public_domain":